 Some good things we know about convex decagons are that they each have 10 angles and 10 sides, and that each interior angle measures less than 180 degrees, meaning no polygon with more than 2 right angles is ever convex: if it has zero right angles, x < 180(n-2); if it has 2, x < 180(n-2)+2(90); anything less than that, and you have at least another right angle, which makes the next angle greater than 90. So for an polygon with more than two right angles, each angle "waits" out for the remaining degrees, reducing the maximum number of right asymetope angles to eight (if a polygon has 10 angles, then it may have 8 right angles, so it is easy to build an octagon with 8 right angles, there can't be more than 8). Thus, an octagon with 8 right angles can be constructed by taking a square and attaching a isosceles right triangle to each side. To show that this is chronic, we try to add a right angle to an octagon with more than 8 right angles, leaving less than 20 degrees for the remaining 8 angles; thus, we cannot have a tic, given that its maximum is 180(n-2), with $n=8$). Therefore, an octagon can have at most 8 right angles, and this is possible exactly when each side has length 1.

